rough 10 sql items that i think are  important  and obviously this is not going to be a  comprehensive list  for every single data science job on the  planet  but for most people i do think if you  know all these items  you're going to know more than enough  sql for your daily job  and who knows maybe even you'll be able  to impress the person who's asking you  sql questions in an interview  but before i do that just a few small  requests  please subscribe to my channel if you  haven't already and also take  one second of your time to smash the  like button  that really helps my content reach a  larger audience  also i will have a link in the  description of this video to my patreon  account  if you're willing to go and support me  that way it would be massively  appreciated  alright so we're going to start with the  absolute fundamental basics  and those are the select select distinct  from  where and order by functions and this is  the part that reads more like the  english language than anything  you select your columns from a table in  your database  where certain conditions are true that's  all there is to it  and then occasionally you'll want to  keep duplicates but sometimes you don't  if you don't want the duplicates then  you just need to throw the distinct in  there  select by default will keep all the  duplicates whereas if you do select  distinct it just eliminates the  duplicates  now the where clause is a pretty  flexible and powerful one  and it's typically going to be used with  a word like and or  or not so naturally these are going to  allow you to create the conditions you  need for filtering rows  based on the observations you have in  one or more columns  but then you've also got the like phrase  and this is going to be super critical  to have  when you're working with text and  strings now you can also use this with  wild cards  and the sql syntax for these are  percentage symbols  as they are in most languages and you  can use these to cut your text in either  direction to filter on matches  then you also have the in and between  operators  now where in will allow a column to take  on multiple different values  where between is for continuous  variables  where you want the value to fall between  one value and another  order buy is an optional addition here  and it's really not always something you  need  just because it does have a tendency to  cause some pretty serious slowdown  if you have data that's on the bigger  side having said that it is a  foundational one to know  and naturally you can use it to return  your records in either ascending or  descending order  so okay you've got your data selected  and filtered  maybe even you ordered it now next step  you've got to summarize it  and that's where the group by function  and various summary functions are going  to come into play  there's all kinds of summary functions  and i'd say the most common one  is the count function that literally  just returns a count of the number of  rows  but then some other pretty common ones  are the avg  and sum functions these are good when  you're working with continuous data  these will give you the average and the  sum respectively  but i'd wager a guess that the  overwhelming majority of the time  you don't want one summary number for  your entire data set  you want to do a grouped summary that's  where the group by statement is going to  come in  and it's going to help you to run any of  these summary metrics  grouped by unique values from another  column  and again that's the majority of the  times you'll need to run these summaries  so get very comfortable with it and also  just remember the group by  comes after the where clause i always  used to forget that  now there's also one more nuance summary  functions  can't be used with the where clause sql  has a different statement  called having for that see this  situation here  where they do a grouped count but they  only want to return the cases where the  count of customers is greater than five  so they use the having clause not a big  deal  just something you gotta remember and  that brings us to another one of the  absolute foundational items of sequel  and that is of course  joins it's very rare that you're just  going to have one  clean table you probably are going to  have multiple tables which need to come  together into one  and that's where joins come in now there  are multiple different kinds of joins  but the overall principle is going to be  the same for all of them  you're going to merge two different  tables together or at times you're just  going to have a sequence of joins to  bring multiple tables all together  and you're going to join based on a  common matching value for a column  that's in both tables  here's the main types of different joins  now inner join  is the default one and that's going to  keep only the records  in which there's actually a match in  both tables  the left join and the right join are the  exact same thing  just with a different order of the  tables and you can either keep all the  records on the left side or the right  side  and then cases where there's no match or  nulls  that's all there is to it and then i can  count on half of my one hand  the instances in which i've needed to  use an outer join  but i suppose that's a good one just to  be aware of too  it keeps all the records in both tables  and there are a couple of other types of  important joins  one that i've used a few times is called  the cross join  and this basically gives you the  cartesian product between two different  tables  it's going to spit out a table with the  number of rows equal to  the number of rows on the left table  times the number of rows in the right  table  and this is a pretty helpful tool if  you're making something like a seed  table  so it's really important to get  extremely comfortable with joins  because these are literally what sql was  designed for  you're creating a merged data set by 