r than the other  i think the most important thing for  most data scientists  is just to be familiar with all the  three approaches  and be able to interchange them quickly  just to tune your query for performance  here's an example of a cte and now these  are created using the with  statement and the thing with them is you  have to immediately  access the results using a statement  like select  you can also use insert or update or  delete  but i think the majority of the time  you're just going to be selecting from  them  and you see here they create one cte  from person information data  using things like first and last name  then they create another cte  from phone number data then they join  the two  on the common business entity id now  ctes are my personal favorite of these  three methods  but again get familiar with all three of  them  temp tables are a similar idea except  you can access them outside of the  context of something like a select  statement immediately afterward  one nice thing i will say with ctes is  you can use them for recursion  to loop through your data set but i do  think that's going to be way more  advanced than what most data scientists  are going to need to do using sql  next up we have two of the most powerful  and flexible functions in sql  and that's case when and exists and i'm  grouping them together here because  together they make an incredibly  powerful combination  casewed is the equivalent of an if then  else statement that you might see in  something like python  and as you can imagine you'll use this a  lot for mapping different values of some  variable  to a different variable like in this  example where they're going from a  quantitative variable to a text string  pretty straightforward stuff exists is  typically used in the where clause  but personally i love using it with case  when  here's an example illustrating how these  two work together  and using this can be a lot easier than  doing joins particularly when you'r