ers and they use those  here to set the data columns  city and postal code equal to values  that are provided when the procedure is  called  so we create this procedure and it gets  stored  and then we can use it with the exact  statement so in this example we've  stored our procedure  we execute it and then we've got our  table  and again this is a simple example but  creating these  is critical if you intend to run a chunk  of sql code over and over again  then alternatively also if you have  multiple users who are working in the  same database and you want to share your  code  it's a super convenient way to do that  next up we have window functions and  these are pretty similar to the summary  functions we talked about earlier  but these are in a class of their own  because specifically what they do  is they work on the other rows of your  table  relative to your current row one example  is lead  which pulls a value from the next row  into the current one  there's also lag that pulls a value from  the previous row into the current one  but my personal window function is row  number  so let's see an example of that so here  the first thing we're selecting is the  row number function  we order by first name and we're adding  a column for that row number  first name is aaron that gets a one  second name is abby that gets a two  so on and so forth that's all there is  to it  now you can add the partition by clause  and that's going to function kind of  like the group by statement  just as an example you could create row  numbers  again for the order of first name but  partition that by  city just as an example number nine on  this list are the useful  pivot and unpivot functions so for those  of you who are familiar with the concept  of long  and wide data pivot and unpivot are  designed to help you alternate between  the two  specifically the pivot function helps  you go from long format to wide  and the unpivot function helps you go  from wide to long format  so think of these like the spread and  gather function in the tidy r package of  r if you're familiar with that as usual  let's look at an example  so when looking at this code let's  actually start a couple lines down  so they pull two columns those are days  to manufacture and standard cost  now the average for standard cost is  taken for each value of days to  manufacture  where that variable is equal to 0 1 2  3 or 4. then the pivot function  coerces this to have one column for each  value  and there's a select statement on the  outside of this just to run the query  then if our data were just presented  like this we could use  unpivot to succinctly just get one row  per observation  so that's no problem either now you  don't necessarily  need these functions to go from one  format of data to the other  but they're going to make your life way  easier all right and for the last item  on this list  we have one that's purely performance  oriented  so it will happen if you're working with  large data sets  and particularly if you're doing things  like joins or sub queries  that your code can take several minutes  sometimes as much as  hours to run well one tool for helping  with this kind of problem  is the index think of these as like a  lookup  kind of like the index that you might  have at the back of a book  now if you were searching for multiple  things rather than scan through  every single page of that entire book if  you had that  index at the back that tells you exactly  where things appear  that's going to save you a ton of time  and that's completely analogous to what  an  index in a sql query or stored procedure  does  so if you create an index on a column  that you're searching for often  or that you're joining on you might just  dramatically speed up your query  and there's two main different types of  indexes  and those are the clustered index and  the non-clustered index  now there's a lot of difference between  the two and again that could be the  subject of its own video  so here's a good table from the website  giant stride  which i think does a decent job  comparing the pros and cons of the two  versions of indexes  and again i think rather than a data  scientist intimately knowing  all of the instances in which one is  better than the other  the absolute most important thing is  just being aware that both of them exist  to be able to look up information like  this and to be able to use either one  when necessary  and now as i promised at the beginning  of this video i'm going to throw some  resources for learning sql at your  direction  a lot of the pictures that you've seen  in this video come from w3schools.com  and this is an awesome resource for  learning tons of different programming  languages  but it's particularly nice with sql  because you can write sample code and  interact with data directly in your  browser i personally learned a lot  through the book  the guru's guide to transact sql by ken  henderson  and i'll have an amazon link to that in  the description  and then naturally there's no substitute  for practice and experience  and there's some great exercises and  challenges on the website  leet code as usual link in the  description  now once again there are tons of things  that i'm leaving off here  and one example is cursors my best  advice on when to use cursors would be  this  you don't but i really think if you know  all of this stuff  you'll be able to get your data into  virtually any format that you want  you'll be writing code at an  intermediate maybe even borderline  advanced  level you'll be able to get your code to  perform at a reasonable speed  and naturally you'll have the notions  down of writing reusable and  reproducible code  and then you're probably ready to move  on to the next big thing  maybe that's r maybe that's python maybe  it's something else  who knows the world is your oyster so  thanks for watching this video  if you enjoyed it and you'd like to  support my work it's super helpful for  me if you share this video  otherwise hit the like button and also  leave me a comment down below  and tell me how much sql you've used in  a data science job  then i'll see you all in the not so  distant future until then  richard on data
 

